{
  "track": "es",
  "level": "B2",
  "unit": 1,
  "title": "The passive voice",
  "description": "Say what was done, without saying who did it.",
  "explanation": {
    "summary": "Spanish has a passive voice for the same reason English does: sometimes the action matters more than the doer. The structure is ser plus the participle, and it needs a por to say who did it.",
    "sections": [
      {
        "heading": "Ser and the participle",
        "body": "The participle is the -ado or -ido form: pintado, escrito, hecho. The tense goes on ser, not on the participle: el cuadro fue pintado, the painting was made. If you do not say who did it, you do not need por at all: el cuadro fue pintado, and that is a complete sentence.",
        "examples": [
          {
            "es": "El cuadro fue pintado por Ana.",
            "en": "The painting was done by Ana."
          },
          {
            "es": "La carta fue enviada ayer.",
            "en": "The letter was sent yesterday."
          },
          {
            "es": "El problema fue resuelto.",
            "en": "The problem was solved."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "heading": "When to use it",
        "body": "The passive is for when the doer is unknown, obvious or unimportant, and for a polite way of saying who did something: ha sido recompensado por el comité. It is much more common in writing than in speech.",
        "examples": [
          {
            "es": "Se ha publicado un nuevo libro.",
            "en": "A new book has been published."
          },
          {
            "es": "Han arrestado a dos hombres.",
            "en": "Two men have been arrested."
          },
          {
            "es": "Fue honrado por el comité.",
            "en": "He was honoured by the committee."
          }
        ]
      }
    ]
  },
